{title: God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen}
{artist: Traditional English carol}
{genre: Christmas}
{style: Carol}
{flow: Opening}
{key: Em}
{tempo: 108}
{time: 4/4}
{score: god-rest-ye-merry-gentlemen.abc}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[Em]God rest ye merry, [Am]gentlemen
Let [Em]nothing you dis[B7]may
Re[Em]member Christ our [D]Saviour
Was [Em]born on Christmas [B7]Day
To [Em]save us all from [Am]Satan's power
When [Em]we were gone a[B7]stray
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
O [G]tidings of [D]comfort and [Em]joy, comfort and [Am]joy
O [Em]tidings of [B7]comfort and [Em]joy
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[Em]From God our heavenly [Am]Father
A [Em]blessed angel [B7]came
And [Em]unto certain [D]shepherds
Brought [Em]tidings of the [B7]same
How [Em]that in Bethlehem [Am]was born
The [Em]Son of God by [B7]name
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[Em]The shepherds at those [Am]tidings
Re[Em]joiced much in [B7]mind
And [Em]left their flocks a-[D]feeding
In [Em]tempest, storm and [B7]wind
And [Em]went to Bethlehem [Am]straightway
The [Em]blessed babe to [B7]find
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[Em]Now to the Lord sing [Am]praises
All [Em]you within this [B7]place
And [Em]with true love and [D]brotherhood
Each [Em]other now em[B7]brace
This [Em]holy tide of [Am]Christmas
All [Em]other doth de[B7]face
{end_of_verse}
